+    Why it can be maintained instead of recomputed: as long as the graph only
+    loses nodes (hence edges) and `valid` only loses nodes that left the
+    graph, an ok node stays ok until it is removed, and a not-ok ("bad") node
+    stays bad as long as one particular proof of its badness survives. The
+    proof kept for each bad member of `valid` is a witness: one surviving child
+    that is itself bad. Witness chains end at a node outside `valid` (a "root",
+    bad by definition) and never loop, because a witness is only ever assigned
+    when it already has a complete chain. So only nodes whose chain was cut
+    (by a removal, or because its root became valid) have to be re-examined.
